# 📚 Binary Search (Part 6) - Binary Search on 2D Matrix

## 📌 Introduction

Binary Search is not limited to one-dimensional arrays. Many interview problems involve searching efficiently in **2D matrices**.

Instead of traversing every element (**O(n × m)**), Binary Search can reduce the complexity to **O(log(n × m))** or **O(n + m)** depending on the matrix properties.

---

# 📋 Topics Covered

- Search in a 2D Matrix
- Search in a Row & Column Wise Sorted Matrix
- Find Peak Element in 2D Matrix
- Median in a Row-wise Sorted Matrix
- Kth Smallest Element in a Sorted Matrix

---

# 1️⃣ Search in a 2D Matrix

## 📖 Problem

Given a matrix where

- Every row is sorted.
- The first element of each row is greater than the last element of the previous row.

Find whether a target exists.

---

### Example

```text
Matrix

1   3   5   7

10 11 16 20

23 30 34 60

Target = 16
```

Output

```
True
```

---

### Observation

The matrix behaves like one sorted array.

Total Elements

```
rows × columns
```

Apply Binary Search.

---

### Index Conversion

```
row = mid / columns

column = mid % columns
```

---

### Time Complexity

```
O(log(n × m))
```

### Space Complexity

```
O(1)
```

---

# 2️⃣ Search in Row & Column Wise Sorted Matrix

## 📖 Problem

Every row and every column is sorted.

---

### Example

```text
1   4   7   11

2   5   8   12

3   6   9   16

10 13 14 17

Target = 8
```

---

### Best Approach

Start from

```
Top Right Corner
```

If

```
Current > Target

Move Left
```

If

```
Current < Target

Move Down
```

---

### Time Complexity

```
O(n + m)
```

### Space Complexity

```
O(1)
```

---

# 3️⃣ Find Peak Element in 2D Matrix

## 📖 Definition

A Peak Element is greater than or equal to its adjacent elements.

---

### Example

```text
10 20 15

21 30 14

7 16 32
```

Possible Peak

```
30

or

32
```

---

### Best Approach

Apply Binary Search on columns.

Find the maximum element in the current column.

Compare with left and right neighbors.

---

### Time Complexity

```
O(n log m)
```

---

# 4️⃣ Median in a Row-wise Sorted Matrix

## 📖 Problem

Find the median of a row-wise sorted matrix.

---

### Example

```text
1 3 5

2 6 9

3 6 9
```

Output

```
5
```

---

### Observation

Median lies between

```
Minimum Element

and

Maximum Element
```

Apply Binary Search on values.

---

### Time Complexity

```
O(log(Value Range) × n × log m)
```

---

# 5️⃣ Kth Smallest Element in a Sorted Matrix

## 📖 Problem

Find the Kth smallest element.

---

### Example

```text
Matrix

1 5 9

10 11 13

12 13 15

K = 8
```

Output

```
13
```

---

### Observation

Binary Search on value range.

For every mid,

count how many elements are

```
≤ mid
```

Adjust search range accordingly.

---

### Time Complexity

```
O(log(Value Range) × n × log m)
```

---

# 📊 Complexity Summary

| Problem | Time | Space |
|----------|------|-------|
| Search in Matrix | O(log(n × m)) | O(1) |
| Row & Column Wise Search | O(n + m) | O(1) |
| Peak Element in Matrix | O(n log m) | O(1) |
| Median in Matrix | O(log(Value Range) × n × log m) | O(1) |
| Kth Smallest Element | O(log(Value Range) × n × log m) | O(1) |

---

# 💡 Interview Tips

✅ Treat the matrix as a 1D array when every row continues the previous row.

✅ For row & column sorted matrices, always start from the **top-right** or **bottom-left** corner.

✅ Matrix median problems usually use **Binary Search on Answer**.

✅ Learn how to convert between 1D and 2D indices:

```cpp
row = mid / columns;

col = mid % columns;
```

This is a very common interview concept.

---

# 📝 Summary

- Binary Search can be extended to 2D matrices.
- Different matrix properties require different approaches.
- Flattened matrices support standard Binary Search.
- Row & column sorted matrices are solved in **O(n + m)**.
- Matrix median and Kth smallest problems use **Binary Search on Answer**.

